hat shall not be named  this person was clearly lonely and he  often kept me on the phone wanting to  talk data and the peculiarities of the  software that dominated his life  he was clearly a person who felt a  little isolated and unappreciated but  was also fiercely protective of his role  as the gatekeeper of the data  i ended up calling these phone calls and  chats gis therapy  but we were successful  we built a massive database  and some of that data in part became the  seed for the first version of open data  philly the city of philadelphia's open  data portal launched in 2010  i was involved in the first version of  this portal portal and the version you  see here was redesigned recently and  it's become the lovely face of a  thriving community of open data  enthusiasts and civic hackers in  philadelphia  but the city's journey to open data was  hard one  and change agents and city government  are still having hard conversations with  folks who are afraid of what open means  and her mark head one of those change  agents  mark is kind of a superman character a  little bit of clark kent by day a little  bit of superman at civic events at night  he's worked on the policy side of  government he's worked as a software  developer he was one of the leaders at  code for america and in 2012 he was  appointed the city of philadelphia's  first chief data officer  right away mark was put to work tasked  with implementing mayor michael nutter's  executive order  pledging greater transparency through  data releases  this open data policy called for some  big changes  make data available through an open  portal hire a chief data officer  establish working groups  get all the lawyers in the city on board  to review the efforts require each city  department to publish a catalog of its  open data  and require that that data be in an open  format a tall order  recently i talked to mark about some of  his experiences working with agencies in  the city of philadelphia  he did have good success during his 18  months as chief data officer and he was  able to get the city to release more  than 50 new data sets including crime  data police complaint data  and some property data really important  stuff but it wasn't easy  what he really encountered  was  a sense of fear in some of the  government agencies that he worked with  they expressed concern  for how releasing the data might affect  the agency but mostly the responses were  very personal  what will happen to my job  what happens to all the work that i did  will people forget about my work  a lot of people working in technology in  this particular city government saw  themselves as solution developers  they built government websites that help  people find parks learn about trash  collection days pay their water bills  they identified as people who built  these externally facing things  but open data turned that around instead  of the government employees being the  sole creators of interfaces to  government people outside of government  could take the data and do what they  wanted with it  and this scared people in government  what's my role will i become irrelevant  mark said  it was almost a lonely feeling of what  will happen to me now  he was able to get some people to think  about it differently by telling them  that they're now a facilitator a  collaborator a steward of open data and  that these roles were crucial  but the degree to which he was  successful really depended on how long  the technologist had been in government  mark left his position as chief data  officer in 2014 after hitting a very  painful road block trying to get the  city to release property tax balances  hugely important in a city with  long-standing problems with tax  delinquency and collection  mark plans to return to government  someday  to lead open data efforts in another  city  while we were talking mark pointed me to  the work of the sunlight foundation  a good government group that works hard  to get government agencies at all levels  to release their data  a couple years ago the sunlight  foundation issued a report on some of  the reasons why  government agencies and officials don't  release data  they wrote the report based on a survey  of responses that they got from folks  who work with governments open data  advocates journalists  you know even folks within government  that were trying to get their peers to  release data  here's some tidbits clean from the  report  my data is a mess and i'll look bad  it's hard it is hard  it's already public  moving too quickly is scary how will we  go back if we have to  those journalists  they're going to really mess it up for  the city  my data is so complex and magical and i  think this is something scientists might  say too if they're pressured to make  their data open  this is my absolute favorite  yeah  i'm not sure where to start with this  one  freedom of information act so  this statement alone probably made a  thousand journalists die a quick death  the old system works  hackers will do stuff to it so  interestingly enough this is an issue  within some private companies too  i ran a hackathon within a big company  and i encountered this fear of hackers  it was really interesting when a big guy  from the security team at this company  charged down to the event to talk to me  about what we were doing  people might sue us  so this is a legitimate fear this is in  part the reason why the city of  philadelphia wanted the lawyers on board  and in fact shortly after they released  their license and inspection data  the local magazine philadelphia magazine  published a data set of people whose  applications for gun permits were  rejected  those data had their name where they  worked the reason for the rejection and  their addresses  and the city yanked that back really  quickly  it's public data  maybe it should be open  but  this is why humans are involved we have  to make good decisions i don't think the  city was sued about that  so this is probably true for a lot of  corporate data although we could  probably talk about some of the wisdom  in that and there are also cases where  data are required to be private hipaa  for example  it's not my job  my incentives are not to make it open  i'm not rewarded  what will happen to my job will my role  change if we're suddenly releasing the  data to the public  so you can probably sense that behind a  lot of these concerns is fear  and really when i talked to mark i got  the sense that in some of the government  officials he worked with there was this  existential crisis  so  for academic researchers especially in  the sciences you can imagine that many  of these fears are the same but there's  one question that looms larger than the  others  and this is probably hardest on  idealistic young scientists  looking for tenure  you know many of them are savvy about  open data practices and open source  tools like python for working with their  data they want to share it but they're  constrained by a system that doesn't  reward it  so i know many of you are from a science  background i want to talk a little bit  about open science and i'd like you to  meet christie balai  an ecologist postdoc at michigan state  university and a blogger  she's very active in the open science  and open access communities and she's an  instructor with software carpentry and  data carpentry and that's how i met her  we taught a workshop together at the  university of michigan to get scientists  up to speed on data management coding  sql inversion control  christie writes a lot about her work on  her blog and she shares her experiences  generously  one of my favorite posts of hers is baby  steps for the open  curious here are some of the things that  she's heard in her community  interestingly you hear the scoop part  from journalists too  fear that someone will take your idea if  you share your code  pre-prince are you insane  so i i'm not an academic i'm going to  take christie's word on this  so these are some of the fears that  she's hearing her colleagues talk about  i know  open science is is not the same as open  data but i think the discussion is kind  of the same and some of the concerns are  the same  so christy likes to say that there's a  movement in the open so science  communities to do better science by  sharing data and code  it makes sense it's easy to reproduce  someone's research if you have access to  their data and the tools they use for  their analysis  and reproducibility is the key to good  science  christie and others like her as a  community builder you know if she does  even if she doesn't see herself that way  and i've learned a lot about what's  happening in this community just from  chatter in the software carpentry  community and on twitter  where people are sharing their deepest  desires for what practicing open science  in their communities could be like  i like this one expressing gratitude  also at some point culture change and  academia is coming and i'm sure that  people like christie balai will be  leading it  so what can we do to help people conquer  their fears and share their data  i think first this is the most important  thing to remember people are hard  and they need lots of support and  reassurance  this is not a bit touchy-feely  maybe but show me a good technologist  who hasn't been vulnerable at some point  in their career  people are hard collaborating with  people is messy  people are hard in government this is  probably one of my favorite quotes from  my conversation with mark  he's a technologist he's a great  developer but he spent most of his time  hand-holding working with people  listening to their fears  people are hired in academia  this is a card that i recently gave to  one of my dear friends who's finishing  up her phd  if you can't read it it says  i'm sorry you cried in front of your  advisor  technology is complicated code is  complicated data is complex people are  harder than all of this  and i just wanted to slip into baymax  hero  photo  people are hard in big companies too  and this is the the one sticker  that we made on my team that everyone  loves the most and i think it really  captures the way we operate in our in  our team in our company we're big  supporters of open source  we know that it's messy and hard  but we work at it a lot we talk about it  a lot my team leader rackspace an  amazing guy named jesse noller would  probably be so happy if we hugged it out  after every code review and technical  team meeting  that's only if we let him  okay so i want to bring this back to  death  let's go back to our friends from the  action mill  their game my gift of grace was so  widely popular  among all different age groups that they  shifted the focus of their entire  company they renamed their group the  common practice  and they started centering all of their  design and conversation efforts  on end-of-life communication  groups of all ages and backgrounds found  these conversations extremely useful for  dealing with many different kinds of  personal and organizational change  so  drawing on the work of psychologists  medical professionals and communication  experts the team at common practice  wrote a weight paper  and talked about their experiences  putting into practice a framework for  organizing ideas around end-of-life  conversations  they talked a lot about multiple goals  theory in this white paper  and here's where i'm reaching way  outside my area of expertise and the  threat of my talk might get a little  thin  but in the spirit of conquering our  fears i'm going to plow ahead  so in short multiple goals theory is  based on this idea that people engage in  communication in order to achieve  multiple often conflicting goals  research in this area has revealed that  a variety of possible there are a  variety of possible goals that people  are trying to achieve in conversation  but three types of goals appear in  conversations on almost any topic  task relational and identity  the task goals are easy the first to  come to mind when thinking about  communication these are the goals that  come to the surface they're stated  quickly and easily and sometimes they  are directives  so if we could imagine a task related  goal in the open data world it's great  let's get started let's share your data  with the public  the relational goals are less obvious  these are meant to change or reinforce  the connection one person has with  another  you might  look at reconciling differences or  maintaining connections or affirming  bonds so here's this idea that we're  going to do it together  releasing this data is for the greater  good identity goals are the hardest to  understand and the hardest to uncover  during conversations they're about  managing impressions both of the speaker  and the listener and this includes  things like preserving autonomy  maintaining dignity negotiating roles  these are really hard things to get at  in a conversation  and they're offering embarrassing for  people to talk about  one thing that mark said during our  conversation is that  he was really trying a little too hard  to convince people that the idea of open  data was for the greater good  really  he should have been focusing a little  bit more on their individual fears  so  my friends at the common practice  started taking this on the road  it's great to talk about theory it's  great to write white papers but where  they really excelled was running  workshops  with lots of different groups  even some technology companies  getting people learning how to have  difficult conversations a lot of these  focused on mortality but not all of them  and the game and a lot of the  conversations started with questions  like this  which might be a little scary for a  20-something or 30-something or  40-something they really get you  thinking about what you want to achieve  in life what's most important to you  and should i really be paying attention  to all these things that i'm afraid of  i added that one  this is another another  part of the workshop exercise  people found incredibly useful  think of the way that you want to be  remembered really kind of strips away a  lot of the fears and baggage that we  carry even in our work lives  another part of the project was to  really get people discussing what they  valued  what their fears were getting them all  out on the table and in the workplace  this is kind of a scary thing  interestingly vulnerability is kind of a  buzzword in corporate america and i'm a  little skeptical  but i think in this particular case if  you're working with an open source  community if you're working with a  community that wants to go in that  direction  this could be really useful  so what am i suggesting here  that we all have therapy in our  workplaces that we all run workshops  that are very touchy-feely no not not  really but i think the key is to start  with an identity goal especially if  you're trying to convince  people in your community to share their  data and open their data  i'm a firm believer that community  building can get us moving in the right  direction this is why so many open  source software projects have thriving  helpful supportive communities around  them  people like mark  people like christy people like the  designers of the common practice  obviously in many cases  it's going to take institutional change  to release data universities governments  big companies with big organizations  it's hard to steer a ship in a new  direction  so i want to end here  when i first shared the idea of this  talk with a colleague i gave them the  title  death and open data he said death and  open data  i'm intrigued but i'm not sure i  understand you mean like death and taxes  and that's not quite what i meant but i  liked where he was going  maybe open data is inevitable and that  might be a dream  but this is a really exciting time to be  working with open data in government  it's an exciting time to be working with  open data in the sciences in journalism  and it's an exciting time to be part of  a big company like the one i work for  where new innovations are being built on  top of open source cold code fed  upstream  and it's all part of a thriving living  breathing  ecosystem  thank you
 

